      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "passage": "Listeners often imagine jazz improvisation as the spontaneous invention of melodies from nothing. **In practice, a soloist draws on a deep store of memorized phrases, scales, and rhythmic patterns, recombining them in the moment much as a fluent speaker assembles a sentence.** What sounds like pure invention is thus the rapid, creative deployment of hard-won material. This is why even seasoned players spend years drilling fundamentals.",
