      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "passage": "Renaissance painters working in true fresco had to apply pigment while the plaster was still damp, a constraint that forced them to plan each day's labor with precision. **Each morning a painter spread only as much fresh plaster, or giornata, as he expected to finish before it dried, since pigment laid on dry plaster would later flake away.** Close examination of a wall can still reveal the faint seams between these daily sections, mapping the rhythm of the artist's work.",
