      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "passage": "The soaring stone ceilings of a Gothic cathedral exert enormous outward pressure that ordinary walls could not contain. **To counter this thrust, builders added flying buttresses, arched supports that reach from the upper walls to freestanding piers outside the building.** By channeling the load outward and down, these supports let the walls grow thinner and open up space for vast stained-glass windows.",
