      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "passage": "Early kayak builders had no nails or glue suited to a frame meant to flex on rough water, so they bound the wooden ribs and stringers together with lashings of sinew. **Far from being a crude substitute for rigid joints, these flexible bindings let the whole hull twist with the waves and spring back, absorbing shocks that would have cracked a stiffer frame.** Modern boatwrights studying old kayaks have begun to appreciate this deliberate suppleness.",
