      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "passage": "Historians once pictured Bronze Age trade as a series of short hops, goods passing from one neighboring village to the next until they reached distant lands. **The discovery of a single shipwreck laden with copper, tin, ivory, and amber from widely separated regions has complicated that picture, suggesting that some merchants undertook long, direct voyages carrying mixed cargoes.** Such finds imply trade networks more deliberate and far-reaching than the relay model allowed.",
