      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "passage": "The earliest cave painters ground minerals into powder to make their colors, relying mostly on iron-rich ochres for reds and yellows. **Producing a true black, by contrast, often required charring bone or wood and grinding the resulting char into a fine pigment.** Analyzing the chemical makeup of these blacks can tell archaeologists which materials a given community burned and how hot their fires grew.",
