      "id": "text-structure-g81",
      "skill": "text-structure",
      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "passage": "The earliest printed atlases of the sixteenth century were bound collections of separately engraved maps, often assembled by booksellers to suit a buyer's wishes. **In 1570, the cartographer Abraham Ortelius broke with this practice by issuing a single uniform volume in which every map was newly drawn to a consistent scale and style.** Because his book could be reproduced identically and sold ready-made, it is frequently called the first modern atlas.",
