      "skill": "text-structure",
      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "passage": "A large dam must hold back water whose pressure increases sharply with depth, pressing hardest near the base. **For this reason engineers commonly build such dams far thicker at the bottom than at the crest, shaping the wall to match the load it bears at each level.** Where a narrow gorge allows it, they may instead curve the dam upstream so that the rock canyon walls absorb much of the force, permitting a thinner and lighter structure.",
