      "domain": "Craft and Structure",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "passage": "Critics have sometimes read Harlem Renaissance poetry as a single, unified voice celebrating Black identity in 1920s New York. **Yet the movement's poets ranged widely, from those who reshaped the cadences of jazz and the blues into verse to those who held tightly to the formal sonnet and its European inheritance.** Reading them as one chorus, scholars argue, flattens disagreements about form, audience, and the very purpose of art that animated the period.",
