      "passage": "Modern translation programs no longer match words one to one; they convert each sentence into a string of numbers that captures meaning, then generate the equivalent string in the target language. **This shift lets the software handle a phrase it has never seen before, much as a fluent speaker can interpret an unfamiliar sentence by drawing on patterns learned from countless others.** Yet the same statistical patterns can mislead it, producing fluent text that quietly reverses the original meaning. For that reason, careful users still treat machine output as a draft rather than a finished translation.",
